- How would you extend to Unicode or larger alphabets while preserving performance?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to extend Dijkstra's algorithm to count shortest paths while handling ties and modular arithmetic—a common pattern in competitive programming and graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm with path counting augmentation
- Non-negative edge weights, including zero-weight edges
- Modular arithmetic for large path counts and overflow prevention
- Edge case handling: unreachable destinations, multiple optimal paths, zero-weight cycles

## Code Example
```javascript
function countShortestPaths(n, edges, s, t) {
  const MOD = 1e9 + 7;
  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);

  dist[s] = 0;
  ways[s] = 1;

  const pq = new MinPriorityQueue({ priority: x => dist[x] });
  pq.enqueue(s);

  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const u = pq.dequeue().element;

    for (const [v, w] of edges[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        ways[v] = ways[u];
        pq.enqueue(v);
      } else if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t] === Infinity ? -1 : dist[t], paths: ways[t] };
}
```

## Time & Space Complexity
- **Time**: O((N + M) log N) due to priority queue operations
- **Space**: O(N) for distance and path count arrays

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses understanding of stability in sorting and practical merge logic, not just raw sorting.

## Key Concepts

- Stability guarantees in mergesort
- Two-pointer merge and preserving order on ties
- Time complexity O(n log n) and space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSortEventsByTs(arr){
  if (arr.length < 2) return arr;
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length/2);
  const left = stableSortEventsByTs(arr.slice(0, mid));
  const right = stableSortEventsByTs(arr.slice(mid));
  const merged = [];
  let i=0, j=0;
  while (i<left.length && j<right.length){
    if (left[i].ts <= right[j].ts){
      merged.push(left[i++]);
    } else {
      merged.push(right[j++]);
    }
  }
  return merged.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}
```

